# Trailing slashes are deliberately ABSENT from the build-output and
# dependency entries below, and that is the whole of #813.
#
# `node_modules/` -- with the slash -- matches a DIRECTORY only. A
# SYMLINK named `node_modules` is a different kind of filesystem object,
# so it was not matched, and `git add -A` committed one without a
# warning: a mode 120000 blob pointing at an absolute path inside one
# developer's home directory.
#
# The consequence was worse than it sounds. Yarn could not mkdir over a
# file standing in that path, so nine of ten CI jobs died with
# `ENOTDIR: not a directory, mkdir '.../node_modules'` -- and the
# PACKAGE named in the error varied between runs, whichever unpack raced
# to the broken path first, which read as a flaky registry fetch rather
# than as a committed file. It also installed fine on the authoring
# machine, where a real directory exists for the link to resolve to, so
# only clean runners ever saw it. Three CI runs and one wrong diagnosis
# (#811 shipped an install retry for a transient that was not the cause)
# to find.
#
# Dropping the slash ignores the name whatever its TYPE -- directory,
# file, or symlink -- and still ignores the directory, so nothing is
# lost. Applied to every entry where that same argument holds: each one
# names a dependency tree or generated output, so nothing of that name
# is ever meant to be tracked as any type, and each is a plausible
# symlink target for exactly the reason above (running a tool in a fresh
# worktree that lacks the real thing -- `target` and `dist` as much as
# `node_modules`).
#
# Entries that KEEP their slash do so because the slash is carrying
# something: `.yarn/*` and `src-mobile/gen/schemas/` name paths INSIDE a
# tracked tree, where the trailing component is the point, and the icon
# directories sit beside sibling icon FILES that are ignored by name
# already. Re-spelling those buys nothing and would blur what the
# neighbouring `!`-negations mean.
#
# The ignore rule is the narrow fix -- it discourages, it cannot
# enforce. `scripts/check-symlinks.py` is the guard that catches the
# class, and is what would have failed this in review rather than in
# nine CI jobs.
node_modules
dist
target
src-tauri/target
src-tauri/gen
# The mobile crate's `gen/apple` (and `gen/android`, once generated) ARE
# committed: they are the Xcode and Android Studio projects, which carry
# hand edits and which `tauri ios build` needs. Each has its own
# .gitignore for build products. Only the per-machine schema dump is
# ignored, as it is for the desktop.
src-mobile/target
src-mobile/gen/schemas/

# Tauri's iOS API, vendored into each mobile plugin by its own build
# script. `tauri_plugin::Builder::new(COMMANDS).ios_path("ios")` (e.g.
# src-mobile/plugins/headstate-notify/build.rs:26) copies the Swift
# `tauri-api` package into `<plugin>/.tauri/` so the plugin's Swift
# sources have something to compile against. Generated, never authored:
# upstream's sources, re-copied whenever the build script runs.
#
# Un-ignored, it broke `scripts/check-privacy.sh` permanently on any
# machine that had built for iOS, which is the whole of #848. The guard
# refuses to scan while untracked files exist -- correctly, since `git
# grep` cannot see them and "clean" would be a scan that never looked
# (check-privacy.sh:57-63). So the leak guard went from "runs" to
# "exits 2 forever" after one `make check-mobile-ios`, and #833 would
# have wired that exit 2 into `make lint` for everyone.
#
# Measured here rather than taken from #848, which understates it twice:
# it is the IOS-TARGET check that vendors these, not every `cargo
# check` (a host `cargo check -p tauri-plugin-headstate-notify`, with
# build.rs forced to re-run, generates nothing), and THREE plugins
# generate one, not one -- headstate-notify, headstate-keys and
# headstate-refresh, 11 untracked files from the notify plugin alone.
#
# Matched by name without a trailing slash, per the #813 argument at the
# top of this file: nothing of this name is ever meant to be tracked as
# any type. Ignoring it is the entire fix -- the guard's refusal reads
# `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard`, which already excludes
# ignored files, so no change to the guard is needed or wanted.
.tauri

coverage
.DS_Store

.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/releases

# tsc -b build-mode artifacts
#
# `node_modules/.tmp/` used to be listed here as well. It is redundant
# now that `node_modules` is ignored by name: git never descends into an
# ignored directory, so a path beneath it cannot be reached to be
# matched. Left in, it would read as though something about `.tmp`
# needed saying separately, which it does not (#813).
*.tsbuildinfo

# `yarn tauri icon` (run via `make icons`) generates Windows/iOS/Android
# Icon variants this desktop app never uses. The Makefile prunes them, but
# ignore them too as a backstop in case that step is skipped.
#
# icon.ico is NOT here any more: tauri_build embeds it as a Windows
# resource, so without it the build script fails before compiling a line
# of app code. It was ignored while this was macOS-only, which is exactly
# what made the first Windows CI run fail.
src-tauri/icons/android/
src-tauri/icons/ios/
src-tauri/icons/StoreLogo.png
src-tauri/icons/Square*.png
src-tauri/icons/64x64.png

# Scratch workspace for plan execution (briefs, reports, ledgers).
.superpowers/

# Git worktrees for parallel branches
.worktrees/

# The Android upload keystore credentials the release workflow writes for
# one build. gen/android carries its own .gitignore for this once it is
# committed; this is the backstop for the window before that.
src-mobile/gen/android/keystore.properties

# Python bytecode. `scripts/check-tauri-versions.test.py` imports the
# guard by path, so running the tests locally creates this next to the
# scripts; #556 committed one by accident.
__pycache__
*.pyc

# cargo-mutants output (see the `mutants` target); `--in-place` leaves it
# in the source tree rather than a temp dir.
mutants.out/
mutants.out.old/
